    {
      "text": "Name:\nRoger Wilson, Nancy Wilson's husband, retired conservative politician\n\nType:\nCharacter\n\nDescription:\nRoger Wilson is a man in his late sixties, projecting an air of old-money, East Coast establishment. He is typically dressed in impeccably tailored, though slightly dated, suits. His face is a roadmap of his career, with jowly features, a perpetually disapproving frown, and thinning, neatly combed silver hair. He carries himself with a rigid, authoritative posture that seems at odds with his retired status, as if he still expects a staffer to be taking notes. He is often seen trailing slightly behind his wife, Nancy, his expression a mixture of boredom and mild irritation.\n\nBackground:\nA former state senator and one-time gubernatorial candidate, Roger Wilson built a long career on a platform of traditional family values, fiscal conservatism, and a hardline stance on crime. His political rhetoric was famously fire-and-brimstone, decaying the moral decay of modern society and championing the \"silent majority.\" Since his retirement, he has settled into a life of comfortable obscurity, attending country club functions and charity galas with Nancy. He maintains his conservative views but is now largely removed from the political arena, which he views with increasing disdain. His presence at the party is a complete anomaly, likely a result of his wife's social maneuvering or a misguided attempt to appear \"in touch\" that has gone horribly awry.\n\nSpecial Traits:\n*Political Hypocrisy: Despite his public persona as a champion of moral rectitude, Roger possesses a deep-seated, voyeuristic curiosity about the very lifestyles he condemned. He is simultaneously disgusted and titillated by scenes of hedonism and depravity.\n*The Wilsons: He is almost always accompanied by his wife, Nancy. Their dynamic is one of social dominance and quiet desperation; she is the driving force behind their public life, while he provides the gravitas and a respectable name. He is utterly beholden to her social ambitions.\n*The Grocery Store Encounter: He and Nancy recently had a brief, awkward encounter with the party's host at a high-end grocery store. In a rare, uncharacteristic moment of trying to seem relatable, Roger mentioned they might stop by the party, a comment he almost certainly regretted the moment it left his mouth and assumed was forgotten.\n*Hidden Desire: Beneath the stern exterior lies a repressed desire for submission and a fascination with Black male dominance, a shameful secret that directly contradicts his entire life's work and public identity. This party represents the ultimate collision of his public facade and his private fantasies.",
      "contextConfig": {
        "prefix": "",
        "suffix": "\n",
